The digits can be reversed with `% 10` and integer division. I save the original value, remove digits from a working copy, and build the reverse one digit at a time.

A negative input is rejected first. The value `0` also works because the loop is skipped and both the original and reverse are zero. This version changes only the local copy, not the method argument.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    bool isPalindrome(int n) {
        if (n < 0) {
            return false;
        }

        int original = n;
        long long reversed = 0;
        int current = n;

        while (current > 0) {
            reversed = reversed * 10 + current % 10;
            current /= 10;
        }

        return reversed == original;
    }
};
```

Dry run for `121`: the reverse becomes `1`, then `12`, then `121`, which matches the saved value.

Time complexity: `O(d)`.
Space complexity: `O(1)`.

Reversing only the second half avoids building a full reversed integer. The loop stops when the reversed half catches up with the remaining half. For an odd number of digits, the extra middle digit is removed before comparing.

For example, with `12321`, the remaining part becomes `12` and the reversed part becomes `123`. Dividing the latter by 10 gives `12`, so the number passes.

```java
class Solution {
    public boolean isPalindrome(int n) {
        if (n < 0 || (n % 10 == 0 && n != 0)) {
            return false;
        }

        int reversedHalf = 0;

        while (n > reversedHalf) {
            reversedHalf = reversedHalf * 10 + n % 10;
            n /= 10;
        }

        return n == reversedHalf || n == reversedHalf / 10;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(d)`, with about half the digits processed.
Space complexity: `O(1)`.

Instead of making a reverse, this checks matching digits from the outside inward. A divisor finds the first digit, while `% 10` finds the last digit. After checking them, both digits are removed and the divisor is reduced by two places.

The input parameter is not changed. The local variable `current` is reduced during the checks. Numbers below zero and numbers such as `10` are rejected because their outside digits cannot match.

```csharp
public class Solution
{
    public bool IsPalindrome(int n)
    {
        if (n < 0)
        {
            return false;
        }

        int current = n;
        int divisor = 1;

        while (current / divisor >= 10)
        {
            divisor *= 10;
        }

        while (current > 0)
        {
            int firstDigit = current / divisor;
            int lastDigit = current % 10;

            if (firstDigit != lastDigit)
            {
                return false;
            }

            current = (current % divisor) / 10;
            divisor /= 100;
        }

        return true;
    }
}
```

The zero case is handled as well, since it has no unequal pair of digits.

Time complexity: `O(d)`.
Space complexity: `O(1)`.

This one uses recursion to compare the first and last digit, then calls the helper on the number between them. The divisor tells the helper where the first digit is. Once fewer than two digits remain, the check is finished.

For `1221`, the outer pair is `1` and `1`, then the helper checks `2` and `2`. A mismatch returns immediately. Only local integer values are used, although recursion adds a call for each pair of digits.

```go
func isPalindrome(n int) bool {
    if n < 0 {
        return false
    }

    divisor := 1
    for n/divisor >= 10 {
        divisor *= 10
    }

    return checkDigits(n, divisor)
}

func checkDigits(value int, divisor int) bool {
    if divisor < 10 {
        return true
    }

    firstDigit := value / divisor
    lastDigit := value % 10
    if firstDigit != lastDigit {
        return false
    }

    inside := (value % divisor) / 10
    return checkDigits(inside, divisor/100)
}
```

Time complexity: `O(d)` for `d` digits.
Space complexity: `O(d)` because of the recursive call stack.

## Solution
```java[]
class Solution {
    public boolean isPalindrome(int n) {
        int input = n;
        int result = 0;
        while (input != 0) {
            int lastDigit = input % 10;
            result = result * 10 + lastDigit;
            input = input / 10;
        }
        return result == n;
    }
}
```

